    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait, \"spooking\"? i haven't seen that term in my textbook. is that a real cryptographic term or did u mean something else? also can u explain more about that part where u said it makes it \"non-repeatable\"? i'm confused about how that works with the hashing algorithm itself.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Spooking\" is not a standard cryptographic term. I'm not sure where I got it from, but I can explain the concept better.\n\nIn practice, what I meant to describe is \"hash stretching\" or \"key stretching,\" which involves adding noise or modifying the password before hashing to make it more computationally expensive and non-repeating. This is done using algorithms likePBKDF2 (Password-Based Key Derivation Function 2) or Argon2.\n\nAs for non-repeatability, I was referring to the concept that the hash output depends on both the input (password) and the algorithm used. Normally, a hash function takes an input and produces a fixed-size output ( hashing is deterministic). However, when a salt is added, the output hash becomes dependent on the unique string of characters from the salt, making the output hash distinct for each possible password-salt combination, even if two people use the same password.\n\nIn more technical terms, hash stretching algorithms use a combination of the following factors to slow down and make the hashing process non-repeating:\n\n- Iterations ( repeated calls to the hashing algorithm)\n- Computational resources (e.g., processor power)\n- Input (password and salt)\n- Algorithm characteristics (e",
